  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0120_0864e54b139f.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 187px;\" /></p><p>Bromine undergo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math> hybridization and the shape of the molecule is T-shape. Each fluorine is having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> lone pairs and bromine is having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> lone pairs. Hence, total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math> lone pairs are there.</p>"
